    "description": "Iranian football combines a long continental record with club rivalries and successive generations of national-team players. Its history also includes women’s competitive development and a continuing struggle over equal access to spectatorship.",
    "tags": ["Iranian football", "Team Melli", "Asian Cup", "World Cup", "Tehran derby", "Women’s football"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T06:58:35Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T06:58:35Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Football in Iran: Team Melli, Clubs and Women’s Football",
      "description": "Discover Iran’s Asian Cup history, World Cup experiences, Tehran club rivalry, women’s football, and the documented debate over stadium access."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "continental-foundations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A continental foundation",
        "content": "Iran’s men’s national football team is commonly called Team Melli, meaning the national team. Its early continental achievements remain central to its sporting history: Iran won the Asian Cup as host in 1968, retained it in Thailand in 1972, and won again at home in 1976. These were consecutive championships, establishing a period of dominance rather than a single isolated triumph.[s1]\n\nThe Asian Cup and the World Cup answer different competitive questions. The former determines a continental champion; the latter brings teams into a global tournament. Iran first appeared at the men’s World Cup in Argentina in 1978. Its first finals victory came against the United States in 1998, when Hamid Estili and Mehdi Mahdavikia scored in a 2–1 win. The long interval between those milestones is an important part of the story.[s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "world-cup-experience",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "World Cup progress and narrow margins",
        "content": "A single match can change the interpretation of a tournament. At Qatar 2022, Iran defeated Wales 2–0 with late goals from Rouzbeh Cheshmi and Ramin Rezaeian. Yet victory did not guarantee progression: the final Group B table placed Iran third, with three points, behind England and the United States.[s3][s4]\n\nThe 2026 campaign produced another group-stage exit. The Asian Football Confederation reported a 1–1 draw with Egypt in Seattle, leaving Iran third in Group G while it awaited the ranking of third-placed teams. Subsequent Associated Press reporting confirmed elimination after draws against New Zealand, Belgium, and Egypt. The distinction between those reports matters: finishing the group did not immediately determine the final qualification outcome.[s5][s6]\n\nTogether, these campaigns show why qualification, individual victories, and advancement should be described separately. They are different achievements, and each tournament’s format determines how results translate into progression.",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4", "s5", "s6"]
      },
      {
        "id": "club-allegiances",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The club game",
        "content": "National-team football is only one route into the subject. The rivalry between Tehran clubs Esteghlal and Persepolis supplies a recurring focus for domestic supporters. The Asian Football Confederation’s historical profile traces Esteghlal to a cycling club founded in 1945 under the name Docharkhe Savaran, or The Cyclists. It later became Taj, meaning Crown, before adopting Esteghlal, meaning Independence, in 1979.[s7]\n\nThat changing name is a reminder that club histories carry institutional and political contexts as well as results. The derby offers an accessible introduction to Iranian club football, but two capital-city teams cannot represent the histories of every club or region. Understanding the domestic game requires following competitions and local allegiances beyond this particular rivalry.[s7]",
        "source_ids": ["s7"]
      },
      {
        "id": "women-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Women as players and spectators",
        "content": "Women’s football has its own competitive history. Iran qualified for its first Women’s Asian Cup in 2022 after a penalty-shootout victory over Jordan. An All India Football Federation preview records goalkeeper Zohreh Khoudaei saving two penalties in that qualifier. The milestone concerns the women’s national team and should not be presented as an extension of the men’s record.[s8]\n\nSpectator access is a related but separate issue. Associated Press reporting on women attending the December 2023 Tehran derby described both a significant opening and activists’ continuing concerns about access. That episode supports a dated account of change, rather than a blanket statement that restrictions had ended everywhere.[s9]\n\nIranian football is consequently best approached through several connected histories: continental achievement, World Cup competition, club allegiance, women’s playing opportunities, and access to public sporting space. Each reveals something that match scores alone leave unexplained.",
        "source_ids": ["s8", "s9"]
      }
